hi there this is steve kaufman here  again with the third in my  summer sessions uh remember that i ask  my viewers to send me questions  via twitter with the hashtags ask steve  and i try to answer a couple of those  questions every week  this week first of all i'm just back  from romania had a fabulous trip there  it was very brief i was able to speak  romanian i was very well received  because i spoke romanian  and i will be doing an interview in  romanian with subtitles next week with  one of my romanian tutors and we will  talk about my visit to romania but today  i'm going to combine three questions  that i received in sort of one answer  and the questions that i received were  one how do i go about choosing these  pattern sentences that i used for  learning romanian second question was  how do i learn grammar and the third  question was what do i use besides link  so i want to combine these to some  extent  so uh with regard to grammar to me  grammar is  largely a matter of patterns  so that i find it  easier  to see lots of examples of the different  grammatical patterns rather than trying  to piece together  an understanding based on an explanation  of the rules  and the more explanations i read the  more kind of confused i get that's not  entirely true but to a large extent  whereas if i can see examples patterns  things become clearer to me so i brought  some it's a little bit of a reach here  but  for example in learning romanian i was  able to find this grammar book which i  can't even remember what it was called  but  it was just full of examples so that for  every you can't really see it very well  but for every pattern of the language  there were lots and lots of examples and  so i read this over and over again i go  back to it as a resource another  question that was asked was how much  time do i spend on the details of the  language versus just listening and  reading this was with specific reference  to learning the chinese characters but i  think it applies to everything you have  the  bits and pieces the grammar rules the  uh reviewing of word lists uh learning  of characters versus the sort of general  uh  you know input reading and listening and  i think as a general rule it's about 70  to 80 percent input 20 to 30 percent  bits and pieces  um details and eventually more and more  output but getting back to this idea of  grammar  to me grammar requires you to have a lot  of exposure to  patterns  uh you can find these patterns in your  listening and reading as you develop  your ability to notice  but it's often useful to see a sort of  dedicated list of these patterns there  was a russian book that i found 53  models  of russian usage  as i say this one romanian grammar book  had lots of examples  again and so here you'll notice that as  i make as i'm answering the question  about grammar and where to find patterns  i'm also dealing with this issue of what  do i use besides link  i tend to buy starter books  like everybody else i shouldn't left  these things so far away just a second  here excuse me  ah boy  a bit of a slow start with all the jet  lag and everything coming back from  romania  but hey you're getting us live so you  know i'll buy this uh you know teach  yourself  or  colloquial  but and i'll work my way through them  but  i continue to refer to them because they  have they sort of touch on the grammar  but the big advantage with these books  is that they have  questions and drills in every chapter  which i don't do  but if you look at the answers in the  back of the book you tend to get a  concentrated list of  sample patterns because they'll tend to  want to drill you on one thing at a time  so i like to  you know touch briefly okay in chapter  eight they talked about  whatever it was the subjunctive or  something and then  the grammar explanation i kind of get a  little foggy on but if i go to the back  of the book i'll see 10 answers to the  questions and so then that's drilling  that pattern into me  so if i vary a certain amount of looking  at the rule and explanation even more  looking at the patterns concentrated  dosage of different patterns  if i vary that with  general listening and reading sort of a  30 to 70 ratio then slowly i start to  get used to grammar so that's how i deal  with the grammar and you know there are  other books for example  uh in korean this was an excellent book  uh called the continuing korean by ross  king  out of university of british columbia  and there are lots of sample  sentences so you just gotta you know get  that concentrated dosage of these  patterns the different grammatical  patterns  and then  try to look for them when you're  listening and reading  some of the grammatical explanations and  the terms used in explaining grammar  particularly in korean i just find i i  can't relate to  but  i think i believe the brain will pick up  on patterns  uh if we get enough exposure to the  patterns both in uh concentrated dosage  through these these lists  uh and by looking at some of the  explanations  all of those things looking at patterns  of the language in use i find uh i get  better retention than if i look at  something like this for example i've i  have bought these books in the past but  i find them very difficult i list you  know i keep it by the toilet or  something you know i kind of review it  but it doesn't sink in  and yet i admit i still go back and i  look at the tables of the verbs but if i  can see them in usage  uh  patterns of them in usage  then that that's much better so now this  getting back to this idea of patterns uh  patterns in the language i was asked how  do you come up with the patterns for  example the patterns that i  uh used for romanian remember that i  wrote out about  150 200 sentences divided into 20 or so  different patterns which i had someone  in romania translate and record for me  and so i mean i think there we just have  to use our imagination how do we select  those patterns think of something that  you would like to be able to say in the  language think of the patterns like  if and because and since and when and  therefore and however and  and why and  these are all the kinds of  key words that trigger certain patterns  in every language tenses is another i  mean in every language we have to  explain purpose cause and effect the  future the past  other relationships  uh so that you know if we even  come up with them in  english and then have them um you know  translated and recorded i think that's  tremendously useful it's something that  we're trying to do more of at link with  our new exchange set up there to get  people to to put up  the typical patterns of things that they  would like to say and to have them  translated and recorded i know that  moses mccormick has a list of what he  considers to be sort of the key  uh  patterns or the key questions and  answers in learning a language so i  think coming up with with patterns is  not that difficult to do  not all  patterns like you'll have patterns in  japanese that  don't necessarily match the patterns and  say english  so that to some extent you have to find  patterns that are specific to a language  because all languages have their own  very specific unique unique ways of  saying certain things  and what might be one pattern in  language a might be two or three  patterns in language b uh you know we  don't have for example uh the same  concept of uh  you know active and past or at least  perfective and imperfective aspects of  verbs that the russians have so you have  to focus on some of those patterns  and so on so  to summarize here because i've rambled a  bit  the question was how should you study  grammar how do i select patterns for  these pattern sentences that i use and  what material do i use outside link so  to summarize i believe that to learn  grammar you need to spend  perhaps you know fifteen twenty percent  of your time  dealing with these sort of nuts and  bolts of the language uh specifically  grammar but that time should be split  between looking at the explanation but  all but maybe and not maybe but  certainly more than that getting  concentrated doses of these patterns how  do you find the patterns well if you  have a book like a teach yourself or one  of those and most books where they have  drills and questions if you go to the  answer section you'll find a  concentrated dosage of patterns  so that's one way of getting patterns  the other way is to just come up with  you know uh 20 or 30  types of things that you feel that you  would like to say in the language and  ask someone to translate and record it  for you something that you can do  there's a number of websites where you  can do that including of course  link and in terms of material outside  link that i use  i tend to get myself a starter book when  i'm getting started i find that useful  and as soon as i can i start to read in  the language so very early i was reading  about central european  um  history in czech  uh  i'm on the internet scouring for  uh you know  radio programs with transcripts so that  i want to avail myself of  authentic content which is in a sense  outside link but i bring it into link in  order to be able to read it so i do use  a lot of material outside link  a to get me started and b as  you know meaningful content that i can  import into link  so a bit of a ramble and i hope that i  touched on those three uh subjects uh in  response to questions that i received  next week i'm going to talk about  fluency what fluency means and how one  can achieve fluency  uh how how quickly can one achieve  fluency and also comparing uh working  towards fluency in the country where the  language is spoken and away from where  the language is spoken so we'll see you  next week and thank you for listening

